  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How Much Tile Do I Need for a Shower?</h1>
    <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mb-6">Published on July 19, 2026 • 6 min read</div>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Tiling a custom shower is one of the most rewarding bathroom upgrades, but running out of tile halfway through installation is a major headache. Tile dye lots vary between manufacturing runs, meaning a box ordered weeks later might not match your existing walls perfectly.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        To order the right quantity of tile, you must separate your shower into distinct zones: vertical wall surfaces, sloped shower floor pan, entrance curb, and recessed storage niches. Each area requires its own square footage calculation and waste percentage.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-blue-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Math: Use Our Free Shower Tile Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-800 mb-4">Don't want to calculate wall areas, curbs, and niche cutouts manually? Enter your shower dimensions into our free tool to generate a complete tile order with exact box counts and thinset estimates.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/shower-tile" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to Shower Tile Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step-by-Step: Shower Tile Calculation Breakdown</h2>
      
      <p>Follow this standard Tile Council of North America (TCNA) breakdown to measure your shower accurately.</p>

      <div class="space-y-6 mt-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">1</span>
            Calculate Total Wall Square Footage
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Measure the width of each shower wall and the tile height (from curb top to ceiling or tile line):</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
            <li><strong>Back Wall:</strong> 60 inches (5 ft) wide × 96 inches (8 ft) high = <strong>40 sq ft</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Two Side Walls:</strong> 36 inches (3 ft) wide × 96 inches (8 ft) high = 24 sq ft × 2 = <strong>48 sq ft</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Total Raw Wall Area:</strong> 40 + 48 = <strong>88 sq ft</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">2</span>
            Measure Floor Pan &amp; Entrance Curb
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Calculate your floor area separately because shower floors usually require small 2x2 mosaic tiles for drainage slope and slip resistance:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
            <li><strong>Floor Pan:</strong> 5 ft × 3 ft = <strong>15 sq ft of floor mosaic tile</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Shower Curb:</strong> Top surface + inside face + outside face (approx. 3–5 sq ft total).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">3</span>
            Add Niche &amp; Subtract Window Openings
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">For small recessed shampoo niches (e.g. 12x24 inches), add <strong>4 extra sq ft</strong> to cover backer tile and return trim edges. Subtract small window openings only if they exceed 4 sq ft.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 rounded-full w-6 h-6 flex items-center justify-center text-xs shrink-0">4</span>
            Apply Layout Waste Factor (10% to 15%)
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2">Shower tiling produces heavy scrap waste around plumbing valves, shower heads, corners, and sloped pan edges:</p>
          <p class="bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded border text-sm font-mono mt-2">Straight Grid: 88 sq ft × 1.10 = 96.8 sq ft (Order 97–100 sq ft)
Diagonal / Herringbone: 88 sq ft × 1.15 = 101.2 sq ft (Order 102–105 sq ft)</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-10 border-gray-200" />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How much extra tile should I buy for a shower?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Always add 10% waste for standard straight grid layouts, or 15% waste for diagonal, herringbone, or complex subway offset patterns. Shower tiling involves high waste due to tight corner cuts, valve cutouts, and niche trimming.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">Can I use the same tile for shower walls and floor?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Large format tiles (12x24 or 24x24) are great for shower walls, but floor pans require smaller mosaic tiles (usually 2x2 inch on mesh sheets). Small mosaic tiles conform to the sloped mortar bed toward the drain and provide crucial grout lines for slip resistance.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">How do I measure a shower niche for tile?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600">Measure the inside back wall of the niche (Width × Height) plus the top, bottom, and side return ledges (Depth × Perimeter). Add roughly 3 to 5 extra square feet to your wall tile order for niche lining.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
